at  framework the meme that's been going  around is well hold on a second  if each american not everybody but most  americans  get 1400 bucks and then you get some  money for kids you'll see it floating  around that  only a few hundred billion of the 1.9  trillion  is for coveted this is so  silly but because it's being repeated so  much it's worth understanding  that in the 1.9 trillion you have  for example 160 billion for vaccine  purchases distribution injection  as well as covid testing that's covid  related  it's not cash for people but the more we  vaccinate  the the faster we do away with this  pandemic and the more the economy will  recover  come on guys you're saying that's not  actually for covid  360 billion for state and local  governments to make sure that  they are solvent solvent many states  because of the when you have a reduction  in business activity  states where those businesses exist take  in  reduced tax revenue the states  still hopefully are providing police  fire schools you know all this different  stuff so  as a result of coronavirus a bunch of  different states need money  you're really going to say that's not  coronavirus related  eight and a half billion for rural  hospitals to stay  open and of course those are going to be  important vaccination sites in rural  areas  25 billion to fund child care centers  it that you're going to say that has  nothing to do with coronavirus one of  the issues we have right now  is many parents can't get back to work  if their kids aren't back in school so  you very quickly start to understand  why funding child care which by the way  we don't fund enough in this country  anyway  is related to coronavirus 15 billion to  subsidize child care  for essential workers we need our  essential workers to be working  they need to be working to make money  but  if they have to be taking care of their  kids they can't work and then they can't  put food on their family's table this is  it's coronavirus related why are we in  this situation  130 billion for k-12 schools to reopen  and decrease class sizes  it costs money to decrease class sizes  to open safely  if you decrease class sizes you either  need additional teachers to have way  more classes  or you need to be paying overtime if  teachers are going to be doing two  sessions  however you're going to do it it's going  to cost money people  on the right want schools open people on  the left want schools open safely  here's money in order to do that we  could go on and on and on you know um  15 billion dollars to prevent airline  layoffs  okay there are some who are saying  airlines should be allowed to fail  unfortunately air fortunately or  unfortunately whatever the case may be  airlines are a big piece of  infrastructure transportation  infrastructure you could make the case  let them fail but we're going to be  depending on airlines  if and when things start to improve uh  to  um allow commerce and allow travel to  restart travel one of the hardest hit  industries so  you know you you can make a case on any  of these things well maybe the 15 should  be 12  or something like that but the the  talking point that's floating around  is most of the money has nothing to do  with coronavirus and of course that is  completely and totally untrue  this isn't a new talking point from the  right  and it's not at all surprising that  they're rehashing it and reviving it  right now

